course: dictionary
lesson: 2799
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. «Dispute» is one of these words. As a noun it is a thing, an argument or disagreement: «a pay dispute». As a verb it is an action: to dispute something means to argue that it is not true or not right.
explain: Read the clues around the word. «In ring-tailed lemur groups the females lead, eat first and win most disputes» tells you the females win most arguments, because the sentence is about who wins between the lemurs. To check, put «arguments» into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
explain: Look at the little words around it too. «Beyond dispute» means certain, with no argument at all. «In dispute» means still being argued about. The phrase changes the meaning, so read the whole group of words, not just one.
fact: A dispute is an argument or disagreement, often a serious or long one.
fact: Pay disputes, border disputes and disputes between neighbours are all kinds of disagreement.
fact: To dispute something means to argue that it is not right, as in «dispute a decision».
fact: «Beyond dispute» means certain, and «in dispute» means still being argued about.
fact: «Dispute» can be a noun or a verb, so check the sentence to see which one it is.
exercise: «In ring-tailed lemur groups the females win most disputes.» What does dispute mean here? | A long walk in the forest | A kind of food | *An argument | A game played with a ball
exercise: Sam says, «The result is beyond dispute.» What does beyond dispute mean? | *Certain, with no argument at all | Still being argued about | Lost somewhere far away | Very quiet
exercise: «The players dispute the referee's decision.» What does dispute mean here? | To buy a new ball | *To argue that the decision is wrong | To run very fast | To agree with the decision happily
test: «The two neighbours have a dispute about the fence.» What does dispute mean here? | A new fence | A kind of tree | *A disagreement | A happy party
test: «The price is in dispute, so the shop and the customer are still arguing.» What does in dispute mean? | Free to take home | *Still being argued about | Very cheap | Finished and settled
exam: «The two countries have a border dispute.» What does dispute mean here? | A kind of map | *A serious disagreement | A holiday trip | A quiet friendship
